h at home. They taught themselves how to scratch and mix, and sang self-styled scat lyrics into stolen microphones. Hip-hop, according to urban legend, was the child of a lightning strike and a robbing spree.</p><p>Itâ€™s art from crime, thinks Ray Carney, the hardscrabble hero of Colson Whiteheadâ€™s rollicking Cool Machine, the final part of his Harlem Trilogy. Or possibly vice versa, given that the man dislikes rap music and nurses a sneaking affection for old-school smash-and-grab. For years Carney has led the sort of intrepid double life that would make Spider-Man proud, supplementing his furniture business on 125th Street with a lucrative sideline as an underworld fence. In his stealthy, dogged fashion, he helps facilitate a process he describes as â€œthe churnâ€: the movement of stolen goods through New York. The churn fuels the economy and helps refresh the culture. Itâ€™s the cityâ€™s equivalent of an endocrine system.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/21/cool-machine-by-colson-whitehead-review-final-part-of-the-harlem-trilogy-is-freewheeling-fun">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;AGzXJdYH6z0VJ65l8OJohjlea6wW0iMYeTQ7sKEV4uwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;10GB7aLsRx_gC37ppNI6_1L_hdybG3GJaFBCcsbrYGAƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;On9QLd0MCoD8-3sgAx2_K-FtpTujgrbRFffw8XjFpRwƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;PIsb1p49x6YZrRZpbHU_wJZ8aV2ZDD07nuJDmTq6VMsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;FMOctUrVQdvgKXTR5YBI7d-h09mqGto4ve7dN9O9TQgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WpD8J