ix, and sang self-styled scat lyrics into stolen microphones. Hip-hop, according to urban legend, was the child of a lightning strike and a robbing spree.</p><p>Itâ€™s art from crime, thinks Ray Carney, the hardscrabble hero of Colson Whiteheadâ€™s rollicking Cool Machine, the final part of his Harlem Trilogy. Or possibly vice versa, given that the man dislikes rap music and nurses a sneaking affection for old-school smash-and-grab. For years Carney has led the sort of intrepid double life that would make Spider-Man proud, supplementing his furniture business on 125th Street with a lucrative sideline as an underworld fence. In his stealthy, dogged fashion, he helps facilitate a process he describes as â€œthe churnâ€: the movement of stolen goods through New York. The churn fuels the economy and helps refresh the culture. Itâ€™s the cityâ€™s equivalent of an endocrine system.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/21/cool-machine-by-colson-whitehead-review-final-part-of-the-harlem-trilogy-is-freewheeling-fun">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YuFkkHWuyslK_WS5VL1LqHxC5I1ItM4dm5b_pw8kkSsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx`Cool Machine by Colson Whitehead review â€“ final part of the Harlem Trilogy is freewheeling funƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;35vh8xOAqBF274ST8jvwEu2Zu3vUBEQ6or48ZKi9IOcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;90fcrdhtN8j5iZE6izNMV83_5POK1Ycc3ndfGfCCEHEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxŠhttps://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/21/cool-machine-by-colson-whitehead-review-final-part-of-the-harlem-trilogy-is-freewheeling-funƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;b2UvE9M6P9sT1Nt9AxxwyZSqRyugNby5wzTBmV6DGvIƒdpai